1
00:00:07,799 --> 00:00:12,216
This chapter is about the people. The narrator
has put it off long enough.

2
00:00:12,785 --> 00:00:18,909
It is easy to tell a story like this from the top —
from boardrooms and stadiums and the

3
00:00:18,929 --> 00:00:25,122
inside of one extraordinary skull. It is easy because
the top is where the decisions are,

4
00:00:25,142 --> 00:00:31,126
and decisions make a story move. But an empire is
not made of decisions. It is made of

5
00:00:31,146 --> 00:00:36,361
a hundred and sixty thousand ordinary
mornings, and the people having them.

6
00:00:38,432 --> 00:00:44,143
Her name was Mireille Achebe. She was twenty-six,
from Lyon, and she taught the second

7
00:00:44,163 --> 00:00:49,941
grade at the Third Ring school on Arklay, and she
was one of the happiest people alive.

8
00:00:50,511 --> 00:00:55,997
She had grown up in a suburb where her mother cleaned
offices at night. She had a degree

9
00:00:56,017 --> 00:01:01,317
she had been told would never pay for itself. She
had applied to Arklay on a whim, in

10
00:01:01,337 --> 00:01:06,949
a queue of two and a half million, and been chosen,
and arrived on a ferry at twenty-three

11
00:01:06,969 --> 00:01:12,456
with one suitcase, and been given a two-bedroom
apartment with a balcony over the water,

12
00:01:12,476 --> 00:01:13,207
for nothing.

13
00:01:13,777 --> 00:01:19,290
Her class had nineteen children from seven
countries. The school had a swimming pool,

14
00:01:19,310 --> 00:01:24,823
a telescope, and a rule that no class could have
more than twenty children in it. She

15
00:01:24,843 --> 00:01:30,617
was paid four times what she would have earned in
Lyon. Her mother had a residency permit

16
00:01:30,637 --> 00:01:35,825
and an apartment on the same ring, and had not
cleaned an office in three years.

17
00:01:37,394 --> 00:01:42,692
At eight o'clock every morning, Mireille's class
said the Creed. She had not been told

18
00:01:42,712 --> 00:01:48,134
to do it. She had started it herself, in her first
week, because the class next door did

19
00:01:48,153 --> 00:01:49,989
it and the children had asked.

20
00:01:50,558 --> 00:01:53,677
We are the shelter. We do not
wait for the storm.

21
00:01:54,248 --> 00:01:59,557
If you had asked Mireille Achebe whether she
worshipped Albert Wesker, she would have

22
00:01:59,577 --> 00:02:04,949
laughed at you. She had seen him twice, both times
at a distance. She thought he was a

23
00:02:04,969 --> 00:02:10,467
strange, cold, brilliant man who had built a city
where her mother did not have to clean

24
00:02:10,487 --> 00:02:13,539
offices. She did not think about
him much at all.

25
00:02:14,109 --> 00:02:18,801
If you had asked her whether she would leave Arklay,
she would have looked at you as if

26
00:02:18,821 --> 00:02:21,563
you had asked whether she would
leave her own body.

27
00:02:24,133 --> 00:02:30,383
His name was Danilo Ferreira. He was thirty-four,
from São Paulo, and he was a sergeant

28
00:02:30,403 --> 00:02:32,761
in the Umbrella Security Service.

29
00:02:33,331 --> 00:02:39,659
He had been a military policeman at home, in a city
where that job meant something complicated,

30
00:02:39,679 --> 00:02:45,539
and he had left it because of a thing he had been
ordered to do and had done. Arklay was

31
00:02:45,559 --> 00:02:51,619
a fresh start. On Arklay there was no violent crime to
speak of; his job was lost children,

32
00:02:51,639 --> 00:02:57,098
drunk engineers, and the occasional journalist
trying to get in on a fishing boat.

33
00:02:57,669 --> 00:03:03,207
Four of the six officers in his section had come
from the Shop. He did not know that.

34
00:03:03,227 --> 00:03:08,831
He knew that four of the six were very good at the
job, never complained, never drank,

35
00:03:08,851 --> 00:03:13,605
never asked for leave, and never quite looked
at him when he made a joke.

36
00:03:14,175 --> 00:03:20,247
He had tried, once, in his second year, to raise it
with his lieutenant — not as a complaint,

37
00:03:20,267 --> 00:03:26,143
just as a thing he had noticed. He had said: they're
strange, aren't they, the quiet ones.

38
00:03:26,711 --> 00:03:32,954
His lieutenant had looked at him for a moment too long,
and said: they're excellent officers,

39
00:03:32,974 --> 00:03:39,217
Sergeant. And Danilo Ferreira had understood, the way a
man who has been a military policeman

40
00:03:39,237 --> 00:03:45,143
in a complicated city understands things, that there
was a door in this conversation and

41
00:03:45,163 --> 00:03:46,085
it was closed.

42
00:03:46,656 --> 00:03:52,330
He had a wife on the Fourth Ring and a son of six
who wanted to be an oceanographer. He

43
00:03:52,350 --> 00:03:55,473
never mentioned it again. He was
promoted twice.

44
00:03:58,043 --> 00:04:04,022
And his name was Tomás Reyes, and by twenty
forty-one he was one of the most powerful

45
00:04:04,042 --> 00:04:07,693
men on Earth, and he was hollow all
the way through.

46
00:04:08,263 --> 00:04:13,941
He was thirty-nine. He ran a department of four
thousand people in nine countries. He

47
00:04:13,961 --> 00:04:19,706
had, without exaggeration, more influence over what
eight billion people believed than

48
00:04:19,726 --> 00:04:25,472
any single human being in history — more than any
newspaper baron, more than any state

49
00:04:25,492 --> 00:04:31,237
broadcaster, because he did not own the channels. He
owned the shape of the water they

50
00:04:31,257 --> 00:04:32,242
flowed through.

51
00:04:32,812 --> 00:04:38,619
He had done it well. Pale Horse had been his
greatest work: nineteen days from sequence

52
00:04:38,639 --> 00:04:44,580
to vaccine, and eighteen weeks in which he had taken
the most frightened planet in living

53
00:04:44,600 --> 00:04:50,406
memory and given it, gently, patiently, a
red-and-white umbrella to look at. People had

54
00:04:50,426 --> 00:04:54,893
cried at his advertisements. He had won
awards he had not attended.

55
00:04:56,963 --> 00:05:02,323
At three in the morning, when the building was empty,
he sometimes played the break-room

56
00:05:02,343 --> 00:05:02,629
clip.

57
00:05:03,199 --> 00:05:09,280
Twenty seconds, shot on a phone in November twenty
twenty-six, by a twenty-four-year-old

58
00:05:09,300 --> 00:05:15,242
lab technician who thought he was being funny. Six
frightened people. A man in a black

59
00:05:15,262 --> 00:05:21,621
shirt saying: every great thing that has ever happened
in this world began in a room exactly

60
00:05:21,641 --> 00:05:22,591
like this one.

61
00:05:23,161 --> 00:05:28,601
He had four hundred million views on that file. He
had made it the founding document of

62
00:05:28,621 --> 00:05:34,122
a civilisation. And he sat in the dark and watched
it, over and over, trying to remember

63
00:05:34,142 --> 00:05:39,895
what it had felt like the first time, when he had been
a kid with a hundred and six thousand

64
00:05:39,915 --> 00:05:44,538
dollars of student debt and no idea what he
was standing in the middle of.

65
00:05:46,728 --> 00:05:48,370
He is exhausted.

66
00:05:48,891 --> 00:05:51,260
He's the most valuable man in the company.

67
00:05:51,779 --> 00:05:58,400
Those are not contradictions. I had one like him. A very
clever, very tired man who understood

68
00:05:58,420 --> 00:06:04,759
crowds better than I did, and who could not stop, and
who in the end took his whole family

69
00:06:04,779 --> 00:06:05,395
with him.

70
00:06:05,915 --> 00:06:07,474
Tomás has no family.

71
00:06:07,994 --> 00:06:11,835
No. He does not. Have you never
wondered why?

72
00:06:14,355 --> 00:06:20,279
The Director did not answer. But he gave Tomás Reyes
six months' leave the following week,

73
00:06:20,299 --> 00:06:26,291
with his salary and his stock and his title, and told
him to go somewhere without a screen.

74
00:06:26,860 --> 00:06:32,977
Tomás went to Patagonia. He came back after nine
days, because he could not sleep there

75
00:06:32,997 --> 00:06:33,471
either.

76
00:06:36,041 --> 00:06:42,298
And below all of it — below the schools and the
promenades and the Fourth Ring apartments,

77
00:06:42,318 --> 00:06:48,574
in the keel of the arcology, two hundred metres
beneath the waterline — was Evelyn Marsh's

78
00:06:48,594 --> 00:06:49,341
laboratory.

79
00:06:49,911 --> 00:06:56,109
It had moved from Delaware in twenty thirty-nine.
Arklay Free Territory was in international

80
00:06:56,129 --> 00:07:01,854
waters, which meant that it was subject to no
nation's law on what might be done in a

81
00:07:01,874 --> 00:07:07,803
laboratory, and the Progenitor Initiative had
outgrown a sublevel under a brick building

82
00:07:07,823 --> 00:07:09,966
in Wilmington some years before.

83
00:07:10,536 --> 00:07:16,185
She was forty-one. She had taken the longevity
therapy at thirty-five, and something else

84
00:07:16,205 --> 00:07:21,663
at thirty-eight that she had designed herself and
reported to the Director afterwards,

85
00:07:21,683 --> 00:07:27,078
as agreed, in a memo of four lines. She looked
perhaps thirty. She slept two hours in

86
00:07:27,098 --> 00:07:33,130
every forty-eight. Her staff of two hundred and
seventeen included sixty humans, who worshipped

87
00:07:33,150 --> 00:07:38,290
her, and a hundred and fifty-one who had come
from the Shop, and did not need to.

88
00:07:38,860 --> 00:07:44,456
She had, by then, made ten more of what the System's
panels called Personnel conversion

89
00:07:44,476 --> 00:07:50,267
— Progenitor (human). Thomas Wyatt had been the first.
The others had all been volunteers,

90
00:07:50,287 --> 00:07:55,883
and all been dying, and all been told the truth, and
all been given, on the far side of

91
00:07:55,903 --> 00:08:01,436
it, thirty or forty more years and a brightness in
the eyes and a total, unquestioning

92
00:08:01,456 --> 00:08:07,311
devotion to the Director that none of them experienced
as devotion at all. They experienced

93
00:08:07,331 --> 00:08:08,214
it as clarity.

94
00:08:08,785 --> 00:08:14,863
Three of them sat on the boards of Umbrella
subsidiaries. One was a deputy health minister

95
00:08:14,883 --> 00:08:20,285
in a country in southern Europe. One was the
chief executive of a shipping line.

96
00:08:20,855 --> 00:08:26,295
Evelyn kept their files in a cabinet she had made
herself, because she did not trust the

97
00:08:26,315 --> 00:08:31,755
network, and on the front of the cabinet, in her
small neat handwriting, she had written

98
00:08:31,775 --> 00:08:32,252
a label.

99
00:08:32,822 --> 00:08:34,065
The quiet ones.

100
00:08:36,634 --> 00:08:42,796
Catherine Adeyemi spent three weeks a year on Arklay
after twenty thirty-nine, and hated

101
00:08:42,816 --> 00:08:48,697
every one of them, and could not have said, in
public, a single specific reason why.

102
00:08:49,267 --> 00:08:54,875
That was the genius of it. There was nothing to
point at. The city was beautiful. The

103
00:08:54,895 --> 00:09:00,769
people were happy. The hospital was free and
excellent. Crime was almost nil. The schools

104
00:09:00,789 --> 00:09:06,597
got children into every university on Earth. Nobody
was forced to stay, and nobody left.

105
00:09:06,617 --> 00:09:12,689
There was no secret police, because there was no need
for one; the Umbrella Security Service

106
00:09:12,709 --> 00:09:15,802
spent most of its time returning
lost property.

107
00:09:16,371 --> 00:09:22,444
She sent Ashcombe fifteen reports in four years. She
could feel, reading them back, how

108
00:09:22,464 --> 00:09:28,396
they must sound in London: a highly paid chief
financial officer complaining that the

109
00:09:28,416 --> 00:09:31,198
residents of a utopia were too cheerful.

110
00:09:31,769 --> 00:09:37,452
In her fourth year she wrote one that was
different. It was three sentences long, and

111
00:09:37,472 --> 00:09:43,491
she sent it from a boat, on a satellite phone, twenty
miles outside the Territory's water.

112
00:09:44,061 --> 00:09:50,277
It said: There is no coercion here. There is no
dissent here either. Julian, I have finally

113
00:09:50,297 --> 00:09:56,445
understood what he is building, and it is not a
company or a country — it is a place where

114
00:09:56,465 --> 00:09:59,391
nobody has ever needed to be
made to agree.

115
00:10:01,961 --> 00:10:08,102
He walked the city at night, sometimes, alone, without
security, because there was nothing

116
00:10:08,122 --> 00:10:13,717
on Arklay that could hurt him and because the
Banner-Sense worked better outdoors.

117
00:10:14,287 --> 00:10:19,630
A hundred and sixty thousand small steady lights. He
could feel them all. A teacher on

118
00:10:19,650 --> 00:10:25,305
the Third Ring who had fallen asleep marking. A
sergeant on the Fourth doing a night patrol

119
00:10:25,325 --> 00:10:30,606
and thinking about his son. Four thousand quiet
ones who burned with a light that was

120
00:10:30,626 --> 00:10:35,906
not light at all, but something more like a wire.
And two hundred metres down, in the

121
00:10:35,926 --> 00:10:41,083
keel, a woman who burned so bright that he had
learned not to look directly at her.

122
00:10:43,273 --> 00:10:48,478
This is what I meant. All those years, in
the car, going home. This.

123
00:10:48,999 --> 00:10:49,682
I know.

124
00:10:50,202 --> 00:10:55,963
I could not build it. I had the crowds and the
banners and a continent, and I could not

125
00:10:55,983 --> 00:10:58,886
build one street like this. Do
you know why?

126
00:11:00,407 --> 00:11:03,267
Because you gave them an enemy
instead of a roof.

127
00:11:03,787 --> 00:11:04,752
Yes.

128
00:11:06,652 --> 00:11:12,408
I gave them someone to hate, because hate is quick,
and I was in a hurry, and I thought

129
00:11:12,428 --> 00:11:18,052
a people could be held together by what it was
against. And it held. For eight years,

130
00:11:18,072 --> 00:11:23,165
it held beautifully, and then it burned, and
everything I built went with it.

131
00:11:23,565 --> 00:11:29,749
You have given them a roof. And a school. And a
doctor. And they will hold for a thousand

132
00:11:29,769 --> 00:11:30,168
years.

133
00:11:32,687 --> 00:11:38,036
The Wesker half of the mind stood on the terrace in
the wind and looked down at the lights.

134
00:11:38,725 --> 00:11:39,879
Say the rest of it.

135
00:11:40,399 --> 00:11:46,469
The rest of it is that I was wrong. About all of
it. Not about the goal — never about

136
00:11:46,489 --> 00:11:52,631
the goal. About the method. Everything I did was too
fast and too loud and too hungry,

137
00:11:52,651 --> 00:11:59,008
and it cost me the world, and a boy from a Wilmington
laboratory did it better in fifteen

138
00:11:59,028 --> 00:12:02,089
years with a medicine and a
laminated card.

139
00:12:04,610 --> 00:12:10,224
It was the first and only time, in twenty-one years,
that the old voice admitted it had

140
00:12:10,244 --> 00:12:11,908
been wrong about anything.

141
00:12:12,477 --> 00:12:18,525
And the Wesker half of the mind — who had spent
fifteen years using that voice's failures

142
00:12:18,545 --> 00:12:24,865
as a whetstone, who had defined himself entirely as the
man who would not make those mistakes

143
00:12:24,885 --> 00:12:27,592
— found that he did not feel triumphant.

144
00:12:28,163 --> 00:12:33,712
He felt, standing on the terrace above a hundred and
sixty thousand sleeping people, the

145
00:12:33,732 --> 00:12:39,346
specific loneliness of a man who has just won an
argument he has been having with himself

146
00:12:39,366 --> 00:12:41,498
since he was a corpse in a bunker.

147
00:12:44,317 --> 00:12:48,927
Integration. Host coherence,
ninety-one percent.

148
00:12:52,017 --> 00:12:57,209
It was the first time the System had ever shown him
that panel. He had not known there

149
00:12:57,229 --> 00:12:57,997
was a number.

150
00:12:58,567 --> 00:13:04,682
Ninety-one percent. Two divergences in a year — two
arguments, out of a thousand, in which

151
00:13:04,701 --> 00:13:08,363
the two of them had genuinely wanted
different things.

152
00:13:08,932 --> 00:13:11,553
In the first year there had been
four hundred.

153
00:13:14,124 --> 00:13:17,850
He stood on the terrace for a long
time and did not go in.

154
00:13:18,421 --> 00:13:23,965
He was not afraid of very much by then. But he
understood, looking at that number, that

155
00:13:23,985 --> 00:13:29,402
there was a day coming — not soon, but coming —
when the panel would read one hundred

156
00:13:29,422 --> 00:13:34,200
percent, and there would be no more arguments
in the car on the drive home.

157
00:13:34,769 --> 00:13:39,857
And he could no longer say, with any confidence at
all, which of them would be left.
